      },
      "skos:scopeNote": "borne by: State"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-156-ss-3-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "Prohibit",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Prohibits seizure without practicable documentary form or copying",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "(3) An inspector may seize equipment under paragraph (2)(a) only if:\n(a) it is not practicable to put the material in documentary form\nas mentioned in paragraph (2)(b) or to copy the material as\nmentioned in paragraph (2)(c); or\n(b) possession by the occupier of the equipment could constitute\nan offence.",
      "cml:conclusionText": "(3) An inspector may seize equipment under paragraph (2)(a) only if:\n(a) it is not practicable to put the material in documentary form\nas mentioned in paragraph (2)(b) or to copy the material as\nmentioned in paragraph (2)(c); or\n(b) possession by the occupier of the equipment could constitute\nan offence."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-157",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "157 Expert assistance to operate a thing",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/gene-technology-act-2000#sec-157"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "gene-technology-act-2000.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "gene-technology-act-2000.pdf, p.131",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If an inspector believes on reasonable grounds that:\n(a) information relevant to determining whether there has been\ncompliance with this Act or the regulations, or evidential\nmaterial, may be accessible by operating a thing at particular\npremises; and\n(b) expert assistance is required to operate the thing; and\n(c) if he or she does not take action under this subsection, the\ninformation or material may be destroyed, altered or\notherwise interfered with;\nhe or she may do whatever is necessary to secure the thing,\nwhether by locking it up, placing a guard or otherwise.\n(2) The inspector must give notice to the occupier of the premises of\nhis or her intention to secure the thing and of the fact that the thing\nmay be secured for up to 24 hours.\n(3) The thing may be secured:\n(a) for a period not exceeding 24 hours; or\n(b) until the thing has been operated by the expert;\nwhichever happens first.\n(4) If the inspector believes on reasonable grounds that the expert\nassistance will not be available within 24 hours, he or she may\napply to the magistrate for an extension of that period.\n(5) The inspector must give notice to the occupier of the premises of\nhis or her intention to apply for an extension, and the occupier is\nentitled to be heard in relation to the application.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If an inspector believes on reasonable grounds that:\n(a) information relevant to determining whether there has been\ncompliance with this Act or the regulations, or evidential\nmaterial, may be accessible by operating a thing at particular\npremises; and\n(b) expert assistance is required to operate the thing; and\n(c) if he or she does not take action under this subsection, the\ninformation or material may be destroyed, altered or\notherwise interfered with;\nhe or she may do whatever is necessary to secure the thing,\nwhether by locking it up, placing a guard or otherwise.\n(2) The inspector must give notice to the occupier of the premises of\nhis or her intention to secure the thing and of the fact that the thing\nmay be secured for up to 24 hours.\n(3) The thing may be secured:\n(a) for a period not exceeding 24 hours; or\n(b) until the thing has been operated by the expert;\nwhichever happens first.\n(4) If the inspector believes on reasonable grounds that the expert\nassistance will not be available within 24 hours, he or she may\napply to the magistrate for an extension of that period.\n(5) The inspector must give notice to the occupier of the premises of\nhis or her intention to apply for an extension, and the occupier is\nentitled to be heard in relation to the application.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-157-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-157-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-157-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-157-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-157-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "01c5ea473a42f7a13ee7495cf33c865a1b97a732594ae9505a7b91e8667d6632"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/gene-technology-act-2000#sec-157",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "157 Expert assistance to operate a thing",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 131,
      "values:originalText": "(1) If an inspector believes on reasonable grounds that:\n(a) information relevant to determining whether there has been\ncompliance with this Act or the regulations, or evidential\nmaterial, may be accessible by operating a thing at particular\npremises; and\n(b) expert assistance is required to operate the thing; and\n(c) if he or she does not take action under this subsection, the\ninformation or material may be destroyed, altered or\notherwise interfered with;\nhe or she may do whatever is necessary to secure the thing,\nwhether by locking it up, placing a guard or otherwise.\n(2) The inspector must give notice to the occupier of the premises of\nhis or her intention to secure the thing and of the fact that the thing\nmay be secured for up to 24 hours.\n(3) The thing may be secured:\n(a) for a period not exceeding 24 hours; or\n(b) until the thing has been operated by the expert;\nwhichever happens first.\n(4) If the inspector believes on reasonable grounds that the expert\nassistance will not be available within 24 hours, he or she may\napply to the magistrate for an extension of that period.\n(5) The inspector must give notice to the occupier of the premises of\nhis or her intention to apply for an extension, and the occupier is\nentitled to be heard in relation to the application."
    },
