    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-170",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "170 Magistrate may permit a thing to be retained",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/gene-technology-act-2000#sec-170"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "gene-technology-act-2000.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "gene-technology-act-2000.pdf, p.138",
      "values:originalText": "(1) An inspector may apply to a magistrate for an order that he or she\nmay retain the thing for a further period if:\n(a) before the end of 60 days after the seizure; or\n(b) before the end of a period previously specified in an order of\na magistrate under this section;\nproceedings in respect of which the thing may afford evidence\nhave not commenced.\n(2) If the magistrate is satisfied that it is necessary for an inspector to\ncontinue to retain the thing:\n(a) for the purposes of an investigation as to whether an offence\nagainst this Act has been committed; or\n(b) to enable evidence of an offence against this Act to be\nsecured for the purposes of a prosecution;\nthe magistrate may order that an inspector may retain the thing for\na period (not being a period exceeding 3 years) specified in the\norder.\n(3) Before making the application, the inspector must:\n(a) take reasonable steps to discover who has an interest in the\nretention of the thing; and\n(b) if it is practicable to do so, notify each person whom the\ninspector believes to have such an interest of the proposed\napplication.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) An inspector may apply to a magistrate for an order that he or she\nmay retain the thing for a further period if:\n(a) before the end of 60 days after the seizure; or\n(b) before the end of a period previously specified in an order of\na magistrate under this section;\nproceedings in respect of which the thing may afford evidence\nhave not commenced.\n(2) If the magistrate is satisfied that it is necessary for an inspector to\ncontinue to retain the thing:\n(a) for the purposes of an investigation as to whether an offence\nagainst this Act has been committed; or\n(b) to enable evidence of an offence against this Act to be\nsecured for the purposes of a prosecution;\nthe magistrate may order that an inspector may retain the thing for\na period (not being a period exceeding 3 years) specified in the\norder.\n(3) Before making the application, the inspector must:\n(a) take reasonable steps to discover who has an interest in the\nretention of the thing; and\n(b) if it is practicable to do so, notify each person whom the\ninspector believes to have such an interest of the proposed\napplication.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-170-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-170-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:gene-technology-act-2000-sec-170-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "91d1355064f5cd21d18cf73c6faed34b82c5605d5016fa4ae46d62b6f2acb43f"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/gene-technology-act-2000#sec-170",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "170 Magistrate may permit a thing to be retained",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 138,
      "values:originalText": "(1) An inspector may apply to a magistrate for an order that he or she\nmay retain the thing for a further period if:\n(a) before the end of 60 days after the seizure; or\n(b) before the end of a period previously specified in an order of\na magistrate under this section;\nproceedings in respect of which the thing may afford evidence\nhave not commenced.\n(2) If the magistrate is satisfied that it is necessary for an inspector to\ncontinue to retain the thing:\n(a) for the purposes of an investigation as to whether an offence\nagainst this Act has been committed; or\n(b) to enable evidence of an offence against this Act to be\nsecured for the purposes of a prosecution;\nthe magistrate may order that an inspector may retain the thing for\na period (not being a period exceeding 3 years) specified in the\norder.\n(3) Before making the application, the inspector must:\n(a) take reasonable steps to discover who has an interest in the\nretention of the thing; and\n(b) if it is practicable to do so, notify each person whom the\ninspector believes to have such an interest of the proposed\napplication."
    },
